it seems like R is holding on to the memory allocated to the temporary objects from the auto diff as it proceeds to write out the sampled objects.  i'm guessing garbage collection / unprotecting objects in rcpp doesn't take place until after the c++ objects are returned to the R layer??  all to say, returning similarly-sized objects from JAGS doesn't hit the RAM ceiling within R.

something that would be useful is a user option to pre-select the parameters that will be returned / written to file.   such a feature may be useful for managing memory for running stan within R.

