On 11/12/12 4:29 PM, terrance savitsky wrote:
> Hi, I ran my model, again, after some help from Ben and Bob such that less memory is drawn by the autodiff and I
> employed the 'pars' option to restrict returned parameters.   I still ran out of memory (using R) as stan attempted to
> write out objects to file.  I'm able to store a lot more parameters for the same number of stored iterations in JAGS
> without a problem.  Since I assume both JAGS and stan store the sampled parameters in similar (S4) objects, I'm
> wondering if the storing the last state of the sampler requires also storing the last gradient computation and if this
> may be an issue.  Also, this time when the sampler finished, I had a message indicating the iterations were completed as
> well as the out of memory message.  Yet, the object existed and when I attempted to extract samples, it told me that
> there were no samples.   I did not receive any indication that the sampler encountered issues, however.

Thanks for the report.

You can always write the models to file rather than
storing them in R using the command-line version of Stan.
That just streams the draws to a file, so there's no overhead
at all for number of samples.

I don't know if there's an easy way to read the results
back into a Stan object.

I don't know how we're storing data in R (but I'm guessing
we're about to find out).  The original plan was
to store the data in a compact C++ object and access it
through Rcpp, but that proved to be a huge hassle for saving
and restoring across sessions.  I still have some hope we
could write custom serialization but I'm not really enough of
an R programmer to do it myself and it didn't sound easy after
a query to the Rcpp list.

Because users really wanted to be able to save sessions,
we switched the code to store the data in R rather than in
C (hopefully it's not being duplicated -- if so, we could
probably cut out a lot of fat there).

- Bob

