> Hurdle count models are two-component models with a truncated (at zero) count component (y = 1, 2, 3, ....) for positive
> counts and a hurdle component that models the zero counts. See http://rss.acs.unt.edu/Rdoc/library/pscl/html/hurdle.html.

That makes sense.  I'm more used to the zero-inflated
mixture approach, but this hurdle model's more general
in allowing the zero count to be deflated as well as
inflated.

In Stan's notation, truncations are inclusive, so
T[L,U] truncates to {L, L+1, ..., U}.

We really need general if-then constructions to
make this kind of heterogeneous mixture modeling
easy to implement.  It's one of the next things I'm
going to do.

